  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nAs of 245 PM Tuesday afternoon, a deep midlevel trough was present \nacross western CONUS with ridging across the eastern US. At the \nsurface, an expansive area of high pressure was located across the \nOH Valley with broad troughing across the central and northern \nPlains. This troughing was resulting in an increased pressure \ngradient across the central and northern Plains. Southerly winds \nwere gusting up to 40 mph with the strongest across central and \nwestern KS. Residual low-level moisture across portions of central \nand western KS continues to yield low clouds, generally west of I-\n135. This has held temperatures slightly cooler in the low to middle \n50s. Otherwise, temperatures in the 60s have been noted for areas \neast of I-135. \n\nTransitioning into tonight, the surface pressure gradient will \nremain in place across the state with southerly wind gusts likely to \ncontinue up to 35 mph, especially across central KS. The combination \nof cloud cover and a coupled boundary layer will limit cooling \novernight with overnight lows reaching the mid to upper 40s across \ncentral KS. Lighter wind speeds across far eastern KS will allow for \ntemperatures to range form the upper 30s to lower 40s. By Wednesday, \nan initial shortwave trough will eject from the central Rockies into \nthe northern Plains. This will shunt the aforementioned surface \ntrough into northern KS. Unfortunately, the best forcing for ascent \nwill remain well-north of the area and dry conditions are likely to \nprevail. Southerly winds will gust up to 40 mph once again with the \ngreatest gusts across central KS. Broad southwesterly midlevel flow \nwill remain for the latter half of the week with a western CONUS \ntrough amplifying. This will keep southerly low-level flow across \nthe Plains with temperatures in the 60s Thursday and Friday. \nSoutherly wind gusts may approach 40 mph Friday afternoon, \nespecially across central KS.\n\nThe next shortwave trough is progged to eject into the Plains \nSaturday into Saturday with a neutral to slight positive nature. \nThis will shunt the next surface trough into the region with \ntemperatures dipping into the 40s and 50s Sunday. A few rain showers \nare also possible with the frontal passage but any rain amounts are \nlikely to remain less than 0.25\". Broad zonal flow will persist for \nSunday and into next week as temperatures remain in the 40s and\n50s.",
